{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0126rB5iCojkw5m7PWDWUMNd","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft Reference Gap Analysis\n2\t\n3\t**Status:** ready. The draft already cites the benchmark-subset comparator families (anchor points, tinyBenchmarks, metabench, efficient benchmarking, PromptEval, ONEBench, the learned/adaptive methods and the benchmark sources). Quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json`, so none are flagged. Three targeted gaps remain.\n4\t\n5\t## Missing literatures\n6\t- Extrapolation of benchmark or ability prediction to models outside the source range.\n7\t- Item response theory and Fisher-information item selection in adaptive testing.\n8\t- Model-assisted difference estimation in survey sampling.\n9\t\n10\t## Rewrite targets\n11\t\n12\t1. **intro-strong-target-motivation** (introduction, high, positioning)\n13\t   - Anchor: \"The hardest and most practically relevant case is a new model that is better than all sources\".\n14\t   - The paper's central framing, the strong-target regime, has no citation. Prior evidence that benchmark prediction degrades out of range, or that new stronger models are the main use case, is needed.\n15\t   - Search: benchmark performance prediction extrapolation to stronger or newer LLMs; out-of-distribution or frontier model score prediction; failure of low-rank or IRT prediction under ability shift.\n16\t\n17\t2. **related-work-irt-fisher-design** (related work, medium, background)\n18\t   - Anchor: \"The latent model is an IRT-style logistic model fitted to a model-by-item matrix\".\n19\t   - The multidimensional IRT, MAP ability and Fisher-information item-selection foundations are supported only by an NLP-leaderboard paper and Kiefer's result.\n20\t   - Search: multidimensional IRT logistic model; Fisher information item selection in CAT; D-optimal design for ability estimation in IRT.\n21\t\n22\t3. **related-work-model-assisted-estimator** (related work, medium, background)\n23\t   - Anchor: \"follows the model-assisted approach of correcting a model-based prediction with an observed-sample residual\".\n24\t   - This is the classical survey-sampling difference estimator, but only prediction-powered inference is cited.\n25\t   - Search: model-assisted survey sampling difference estimator; PPI++ or active statistical inference.\n26\t\n27\t## Not flagged\n28\tBenchmark citations, stacked generalization, the D-optimality equivalence theorem and the comparator paragraphs are adequately supported. Adding more would only pad the bibliography.\n29\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"00e439e2-5fe8-4238-8844-beca9ad285e7","uuid":"d298f61c-965e-44a0-ac49-916cd8a1a08d","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:24:30.675Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper/draft_reference_gaps.md","content":"# Draft Reference Gap Analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. The draft already cites the benchmark-subset comparator families (anchor points, tinyBenchmarks, metabench, efficient benchmarking, PromptEval, ONEBench, the learned/adaptive methods and the benchmark sources). Quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json`, so none are flagged. Three targeted gaps remain.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Extrapolation of benchmark or ability prediction to models outside the source range.\n- Item response theory and Fisher-information item selection in adaptive testing.\n- Model-assisted difference estimation in survey sampling.\n\n## Rewrite targets\n\n1. **intro-strong-target-motivation** (introduction, high, positioning)\n   - Anchor: \"The hardest and most practically relevant case is a new model that is better than all sources\".\n   - The paper's central framing, the strong-target regime, has no citation. Prior evidence that benchmark prediction degrades out of range, or that new stronger models are the main use case, is needed.\n   - Search: benchmark performance prediction extrapolation to stronger or newer LLMs; out-of-distribution or frontier model score prediction; failure of low-rank or IRT prediction under ability shift.\n\n2. **related-work-irt-fisher-design** (related work, medium, background)\n   - Anchor: \"The latent model is an IRT-style logistic model fitted to a model-by-item matrix\".\n   - The multidimensional IRT, MAP ability and Fisher-information item-selection foundations are supported only by an NLP-leaderboard paper and Kiefer's result.\n   - Search: multidimensional IRT logistic model; Fisher information item selection in CAT; D-optimal design for ability estimation in IRT.\n\n3. **related-work-model-assisted-estimator** (related work, medium, background)\n   - Anchor: \"follows the model-assisted approach of correcting a model-based prediction with an observed-sample residual\".\n   - This is the classical survey-sampling difference estimator, but only prediction-powered inference is cited.\n   - Search: model-assisted survey sampling difference estimator; PPI++ or active statistical inference.\n\n## Not flagged\nBenchmark citations, stacked generalization, the D-optimality equivalence theorem and the comparator paragraphs are adequately supported. Adding more would only pad the bibliography.\n","numLines":29,"startLine":1,"totalLines":29}}}
