d work\",\n      \"priority\": \"medium\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"background\",\n      \"reason\": \"The 'Estimation and design tools' paragraph treats the logistic latent model and Fisher-information item selection as established, but supports them only with an NLP-leaderboard IRT paper and Kiefer's D-optimality result. The multidimensional IRT, MAP ability estimation and Fisher-information item selection foundations that the method builds on are not cited.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"The latent model is an IRT-style logistic model fitted to a model-by-item matrix\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"multidimensional item response theory logistic model\",\n        \"Fisher information item selection computerized adaptive testing\",\n        \"D-optimal design for item calibration or ability estimation in IRT\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"related-work-model-assisted-estimator\",\n      \"section\": \"related work\",\n      \"priority\": \"medium\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"background\",\n      \"reason\": \"The estimator that adds the mean residual of the revealed items to a model-based prediction is the classical model-assisted or difference estimator from survey sampling. The draft links it only to prediction-powered inference, which is a recent descendant. One or two foundational or bridging citations would position it correctly.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"follows the model-assisted approach of correcting a model-based prediction with an observed-sample residual\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"model-assisted survey sampling difference estimator\",\n        \"PPI++ or active statistical inference with model predictions and labeled sample\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n","numLines":51,"startLine":1,"totalLines":51}}}
